# Vercel Speed Insights Implementation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for this Next.js project following the latest official documentation from https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights@^2.0.0` package using npm
- Updated `package.json` to include the new dependency
- Updated `package-lock.json` with the new package and its dependencies

### 2. Component Integration
- Modified `app/layout.tsx` to add Speed Insights tracking:
  - Added import: `import { SpeedInsights } from "@vercel/speed-insights/next";`
  - Added `<SpeedInsights />` component in the body section of the root layout

## Implementation Details

The implementation follows Vercel's recommended approach for Next.js 16.2.6 (App Router):
- Used the Next.js-specific import path: `@vercel/speed-insights/next`
- Placed the component at the root layout level to track all pages
- Added the component alongside the existing `<Toaster />` component in the body

## Verification

- ✅ Build completed successfully (`npm run build`)
- ✅ No new linting errors introduced
- ✅ TypeScript compilation passed
- ✅ All lock files properly updated

## Next Steps

To complete the setup:
1. Enable Speed Insights in your Vercel dashboard for this project
2. Deploy the changes to Vercel
3. View metrics in the Speed Insights dashboard after users visit the site

The Speed Insights component will automatically:
- Track Web Vitals (LCP, FID, CLS, etc.)
- Send performance data to Vercel's analytics endpoint
- Work seamlessly with server-side rendering and client components
